<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pirna</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pirna is a town on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, upstream of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dresden">Dresden</a> at the entrance to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>. In 1756, at the start of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seven-years-war">Seven Years' War</a>, the Saxon army was trapped in a fortified camp between Pirna and the fortress of Königstein and capitulated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-the-great">Frederick the Great</a>, whose officers then forced its soldiers into Prussian service. The Venetian painter Bernardo Bellotto made celebrated views of the town.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pirna.jpg" alt="Pirna" width="320" height="179" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kalispera Dell · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Human_rights_memorial_Castle-Fortress_Sonnenstein_117956973.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
